# Git policy

Do not run git write operations by default. That includes commit, push, pull, merge, rebase, branch create/delete, add, stash, restore --staged, reset, and amending history.

Allowed always: read-only git inspection (`status`, `diff`, `log`, `branch -a`, `show`) when needed to understand the codebase.

When the user explicitly asks to commit and/or push (or other git writes):
1. Inspect status/diff/log as usual and draft the exact commands.
2. Show those commands in the reply.
3. Ask for permission to run them.
4. Only execute after the user confirms (e.g. "yes", "go ahead", "run it").

Do not commit, push, or otherwise mutate git state without that confirmation in the same turn or a clear follow-up.

Code edits and implementation are fine.
